  "summary_en": "This law review article looks at liability for violating robots.txt, the standard that limits web crawlers. It focuses on the large-scale scraping done to train generative AI. The authors argue that common-law contract and tort doctrines can hold crawler deployers accountable. These include unilateral contract, trespass to chattels and negligence, and the authors prefer them to criminal or copyright remedies. The \"robots\" here are software crawlers, not physical robots.",
  "key_provisions_or_claims": [
   "Ignoring robots.txt causes more harm now that crawlers gather training data for large language models.",
   "In some conditions, robots.txt can form a unilateral contract, with crawler access counting as acceptance by conduct.",
   "robots.txt can serve as notice that supports tort liability for trespass to chattels and negligence.",
   "Civil remedies give a better balance than criminal or copyright-based ones.",
   "Clear liability rules help counter the growing fragmentation of the internet."
  ],
  "confidence": 0.88
